  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1011 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nDeterministic models remain in pretty good agreement regarding the\nlarge scale pattern evolution through Tues. The main forecast trend\ncontinues to be a slower evolution of the upper low that cuts off\nover the SW US on Sat. This slower evolution translates to lower\nprecip chances on Sat/Sun (especially off the Caprock). The upper \nlow will also be weakening in time, so this slow trend means lower\naccumulation probs and lower impacts anticipated compared to \nprevious forecasts. The highest PWAT values will be on the \nCaprock on Sun, with values around 1.15-1.25\", and this time \nperiod offers the best window for flooding with any training \nstorms. By Mon- Tues, PWAT values are closer to 1\", which is still\nabove normal but not quite as anomalous. Latest ensemble guidance\ngives a 30% chance of total rainfall exceeding 1\" in \nLubbock/Brownfield (through Tuesday), increasing to a 60% chance \nat Friona, but both of these values are lower than previous \ncycles. The probability of 1\" of rain is even lower off the \nCaprock. \n\nThis system will result in temps trending cooler, in the 70s area-\nwide on Sat followed by mid 60s to mid 70s Sunday into next week.\nThe breezy southeasterly winds continue through the weekend. Model\nsolutions start to diverge beyond the middle of next week, but \nthe upper pattern seems to stay active with additional troughs \nmoving onshore the Pacific NW, perhaps supporting additional \nprecip chances locally during the final days of October.",
